# Modularity (library → CLI → GUI)

## Layout

| Layer | Location | Role |
|-------|----------|------|
| Library | `src/telemetry.rs`, `src/maintenance.rs` | Core logic: read/apply settings, elevation, startup/post-update |
| CLI | `src/bin/cli.rs` → `windows-diagnostics` | Exposes commands over the library |
| GUI | `src/gui.rs` + `src/bin/gui.rs` → `windows-diagnostics-gui` | Thin UI; calls the same library APIs |

Both binaries are independently runnable (`cli` / `gui` features). Shared helpers (e.g. hidden Windows subprocesses) live in the library (`win_cmd`).

## Prefer this order when changing behavior

1. **Extend the library** (`telemetry` / `maintenance`) with the real operation.
2. **Expose it on the CLI** if users should script it (`status`, `disable`, `enable`, `set`, `startup`, `post-update`, `integration`, explain/list).
3. **Wire the GUI** to call those library APIs — do **not** reimplement registry, `sc`, or `schtasks` logic in `gui.rs`.

## GUI ↔ CLI parity

GUI actions should map to the same library operations as CLI:

- status / verify → `read_all` / `read_one`
- disable / enable / set → `apply` / `apply_all`
- startup / post-update / integration → `maintenance::*`
- logs / open → `system_links::*` (Event Viewer, Settings diagnostics pages, log folders, …)

## Do / don't

```text
// ✅ GOOD — GUI delegates
let settings = telemetry::read_all();
telemetry::apply(id, active)?;

// ❌ BAD — GUI shells out or duplicates policy paths
Command::new("reg").args([...]);
// hard-coded HKLM paths only in gui.rs
```

Keep CLI and GUI independently buildable; never make core behavior GUI-only.
